WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:08.420
Evo Grand Finals matches are some of the biggest, most important matches played for any fighting game.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:14.280
"In all of the years of Street Fighter 4 at Evo, Gamerbee has been the best performer."

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.300
They provide some of the higest prize payouts,

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:21.200
sure. But they also provide a look at the absolute highest levels of competition.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.320
Two of the greatest players in the world,

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.480
facing off with everything on the line.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.420
after having run a gauntlet of some of the toughest competition they've ever faced.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.580
Evo Grand Finals are where the magic happens.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:38.380
(Casting)

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:44.340
And sometimes, that magic is made all the more powerful by something outside the players’ control.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.980
Sometimes everything goes wrong ,

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.700
and the most important match of the year also becomes the craziest.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:01:07.160
(Casting)

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:13.940
In 2015, Yusuke “Momochi” Momochi was on top of the Street Fighter IV world.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:17.700
He had won the 2014 Capcom Cup but he didn’t rest on his laurels.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.640
He attended and dominated plenty of events on the 2015 Capcom Pro Tour

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.380
and off-circuit tournaments.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:26.120
He won Shadowloo Showdown and South by Southwest

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:30.320
and then placed second at Final Round, the Topanga World League and Stunfest.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:37.900
(Casting)

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:41.360
Simply put, Momochi was one of the best players in the world,

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.220
heading into Evo 2015.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:47.620
Meanwhile, a perennial underdog was having a pretty good year of his own.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:52.540
Bruce “Gamerbee” Hsiang narrowly missed the 2014 Capcom Cup finals

00:01:52.760 --> 00:02:08.040
(Casting)

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:11.840
Not being there crushed him and he even considered retirement.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.880
Gamerbee was long-considered Taiwan’s finest Street Fighter IV player,

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:21.720
and he always showed up at Evo.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:28.020
Fifth in 2010, 17th in 2011, second in 2012 and ninth in 2013.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:40.520
(Casting)

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.080
Few players had that level of consistency,

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:47.520
and despite missing Capcom Cup, Gamerbee was still one of the best in the world.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.520
He just wasn’t the international champion that players like Momochi were.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.380
In fact, Gamerbee placed just behind Momochi a few times that year,

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.440
third at South By Southwest and fifth at Stunfest.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.340
He was having a solid year, just not the best.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:04.420
But Gamerbee always showed up at Evo.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.120
Momochi had a pretty uneventful run through the bracket,

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.480
beating Gackt and Xian to make Top 8.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.920
Meanwhile, Gamerbee made a pretty clean run through his pools, with only handful of wins

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.340
over notable players like Dakou and Pepeday.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:22.120
Unfortunately, things came to a screeching halt when Gamerbee faced off against Infiltration.

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:25.700
Infiltration and Gamerbee had something of a history at Evo.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:31.200
Infiltration capped off his 2012 win by crushing Gamerbee 3-0 in the Grand Finals.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:47.160
(Casting)

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.460
And while things were closer in 2015

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:56.000
(Casting)

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.760
They still didn’t swing in Gamerbee’s favor.

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:03.960
Evo’s loser’s bracket is one of the most dangerous places in fighting games.

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.660
You suddenly have to play twice the number of matches to make it to Grand Finals,

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:09.780
and past pools it’s full of sharks.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:13.220
And Gamerbee got thrown in with one of the biggest sharks out there.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:22.800
"Wow this is a match to qualify for Top 8. It is gonna be Daigo Umehara against AVerMedia's Gamerbee."

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.680
"One of these guys is not making Top 8."

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:55.400
(Casting)

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:59.780
Gamerbee made Top 8, but he still had a mountain to climb, fighting NuckleDu

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:07.060
(Casting)

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:07.940
Tokido

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:16.040
(Casting)

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.040
And Nemo.

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:32.380
(Casting)

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:38.160
To earn a spot in loser’s finals, up against the man who put him there. Infiltration.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:43.140
"And honestly Infiltration, can probably be considered one of Gamerbee's demons."

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:47.080
Infiltration and Gamerbee played a grueling, 30-minute set

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:50.720
thanks in part to Gamerbee’s pick, Elena, and her healing ability.

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:56.480
Elena’s Ultra 2, known simply as “Healing,” restores up to a third of her health based on how long she holds it,

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:00.840
and she builds the Ultra Meter to use it based on damage she takes during a round.

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:05.120
Yeah that’s right, she takes damage to fuel her Healing,

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:09.480
which gives her more health to take more damage to heal all over again.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:07:06.120
(Casting)

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:09.100
But after both players switched to more aggressive characters,

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:12.440
Gamerbee came out on top with one last timer scam.

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:38.300
(Casting)

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:42.580
Gamerbee played the longest set of the tournament, but he still had one more obstacle.

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:47.480
Momochi, who was sitting in Grand Finals after beating Tokido and Infiltration,

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:49.660
and was poised to take the entire tournament.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:51.680
but Gamerbee was on a roll.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:08:29.440
(Casting)

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:30.840
For the second set of Grand Finals,

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.680
Momochi swapped over to Evil Ryu,

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:35.240
a character who could dish out a lot more damage than Ken.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:42.400
(Casting)

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:46.780
And while it worked for the first round, Gamerbee continued to trade games with Momochi.

00:08:46.940 --> 00:10:01.540
(Casting)

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:03.980
While Momochi took the first round of the last game,

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:06.640
both players were so close to victory.

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:11.280
with all the pressure in the world on their backs, the unthinkable happened.

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:24.240
(Casting)

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:26.560
Just as the second round of the final game started,

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:28.480
Momochi’s stick disconnected.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:32.220
Evo rules state that in the event of a pause, intentional or otherwise,

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:34.860
the player who caused the pause has to forfeit the round.

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:39.920
(Casting)

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:42.040
But Momochi had other problems.

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:45.220
Momochi didn't just pause the game, his stick broke.

00:10:45.220 --> 00:10:47.400
Which according to his manager at the time,

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:48.580
Antonio Javier,

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:50.820
caused something of a crisis for his team.

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:56.440
"All of a sudden I'm sitting there like, 'Oh my god where are the rest of the guys, I need a spare stick

00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:59.000
I need something that Momochi is comfortable playing on.'"

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:01.100
For Momochi, the issue was his stick breaking,

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:02.320
but for Gamerbee?

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:04.900
He had a ton of momentum going into the Grand Finals,

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:06.360
but he was mentally drained.

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:09.020
He had just played the longest set of the tournament.

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:14.040
And now? He was stuck waiting, taken out of the game, unable to stay warmed up.

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:16.480
"I can't imagine what they're going through."

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:20.600
Back in the Evil Geniuses camp, AJ and Momochi’s partner, Chocoblanka, were

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:23.800
struggling to find a new stick when the unthinkable happened.

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:29.800
"So I jumped up out of my seat ran through the aisles, ran into Choco who was doing the same thing.

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.100
Running through the aisles at Evo yelling, "Does anyone have a Razer stick!"

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:42.340
No one had one. So we went back to main stage after we run through the entire convention.

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:48.780
Not finding a single Razer stick. I see Tokido come up, and give him his stick."

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:53.020
Tokido, who both Gamerbee and Momochi defeated to make it to Grand Finals,

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:54.700
gave his stick to Momochi.

00:11:54.700 --> 00:12:00.520
and with that stick in-hand, after five minutes and 17 seconds that must have felt like an eternity,

00:12:01.060 --> 00:12:03.040
both players got back to the match.

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:05.280
"And the time will now count down."

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:08.280
"Here it is, last round."

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:10.900
"Evolution, 2015."

00:12:14.400 --> 00:13:15.280
(Casting)

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:18.920
Momochi emerged victorious, and Gamerbee, ever the bridesmaid,

00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:19.840
stayed second.

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:23.580
He was comforted by Infiltration, and then his partner Julianna,

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:28.260
who was in tears over an Evo Gamerbee was so close to finally winning.

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:37.420
"And the crowd on its feet for Gamerbee, a lot of respect. He can come out of this in a very good position."

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:40.520
2015 was Street Fighter IV’s last year at Evo,

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:44.200
and while 2016 gave us an incredible Street Fighter V Top 8,

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:48.680
Gamerbee didn't make it. He was put in losers once again, by Infiltration.

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:51.800
But despite that Gamerbee is still going to tournaments.

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:54.200
SFV might not be his favorite game,

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:57.360
but Gamerbee always has the potential to come up huge,

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:00.660
to play underdog and triumph over impossible odds.

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:04.780
Gamerbee is one of the hardest working, most beloved people in the FGC,

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:09.320
and at Evo, even if he doesn’t win, he always shows up.

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:13.320
"Certainly he's earned a lot of fans for his incredible play here today."

